II. Trim Detection & Applicability
Challenge 2A: Trim Column Identification Across Sections

Trim detection must come after header detection is complete. That way, we have established standard column structure.

Since some trims might be on a section not all sections, then the plan will be to process a section at a time and add them one by one to the unified list.

Usually trims are supposed to be identical, meaning we don't expect Section 1 has "CR-V" and Section 2 has "CRV". IN that case it ti will tough to catch, but i want us to be able to catch it. However, using the db as source of through, we could use that to catch anomalies like that since they won't match the original trim name and there for won't get model number if that's the case.

Just make sure that section are consolidated properly into a unified list by vehicle model.

Section-Specific Trim Applicability:

A part must apply to at least one trim in a model family.
This means, it can apply to one trim and not the other on the list.

When we unify them, there will be rows that specify each applicability by each trim, they all will be unified into one list showing the relationship between part and trim. It will start by section then, trickle down to the unified list.

Detecting "X" vs. Blank vs. Null:

The following structure would suffice. in the future if the indicatives change, we can add more characters.

trim_column_values:
applicability_markers: ["•", "T", "E"] Note: case insensitive # Variations for "applies"
not_applicable_markers: [" ", "", "N/A"]

Challenge 2B: Trim Applicability Validation

A part has no trims marked (invalid row) -> it it an orphan record and should be report in the DQ report and excluded in the pipeline, we'll handle it separetly.

A part applies to all trims (filled across all columns) -> very valid, thenn it it will have records for each trim indicated in that section.

A part applies to one trim only -> that part will only have one record applied to that one trim

Minimum trims per part: 1? - > Yes, one
Maximum trims per part: all? -> Yes, all

